<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleopatra VII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/51-bce">51</a>–<a href="/years/30-bce">30 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cleopatra VII was the last active ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>, reigning from 51 to 30 BCE. She allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> in an attempt to preserve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s independence. After their defeat by Octavian at Actium, she died by suicide, and Egypt became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii.jpg" alt="Cleopatra VII" width="317" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis le Grand · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleopatra-VII.-Altes-Museum-Berlin1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cleopatra VII was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in early 69 BCE, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII Auletes</a> and an uncertain mother, presumably his wife Cleopatra V Tryphaena. She belonged to the Macedonian Greek Ptolemaic dynasty, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a>, a general and companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Earlier Ptolemaic rulers refused to learn Egyptian; she was the first known to have done so, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> implies that she spoke several other languages as well.</p><p>Her father tried to ward off Roman annexation by lavish gifts to statesmen such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, which bankrupted him, and in 58 BCE he was exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Before he died, some time before 22 March 51 BCE, he named her his heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Cleopatra began to reign alongside her brother Ptolemy XIII. Her first act as queen was installing a sacred Buchis bull at Hermonthis, and she faced famine caused by drought and a low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood. A falling-out with her brother led to civil war. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after losing at Pharsalus in 48 BCE, Ptolemy XIII had him ambushed and killed, and Caesar then occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and was besieged with Cleopatra in the palace. After Ptolemy XIII drowned in the Nile, Caesar made Cleopatra and her brother Ptolemy XIV joint rulers; her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a> was born of her affair with Caesar. After Caesar's assassination she named Caesarion co-ruler.</p><p>In the civil war of 43–42 BCE she sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> of Octavian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Marcus Aemilius Lepidus. After meeting Antony at Tarsos in 41 BCE she had three children with him, and he came to rely on her for funds and military aid. The Donations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> declared their children rulers of territories under his authority, and Octavian presented this as treason and declared war on Cleopatra. She ruled as an absolute monarch and chief religious authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Octavian defeated the fleet of Antony and Cleopatra at Actium in 31 BCE, his forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 30 BCE and Antony killed himself. Cleopatra reportedly killed herself too, probably by poison, to avoid being paraded in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-triumph">Roman triumph</a>. Her three surviving children were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the care of Octavian's sister Octavia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>'s Life of Antonius gives the fullest surviving account and draws on eyewitness sources as well as the Augustan view. Roman historiography and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poetry produced a generally critical view of the queen that shaped medieval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cleopatra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The reign of <b>Cleopatra VII</b> (r. 51–30 BCE) was the final phase of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of Antony and <b>Cleopatra VII</b> at the Battle of Actium in 31 BCE, Octavian marched on Alexandria, which fell on 1 August 30 BCE; Antony and Cleopatra took their own lives, and Cleopatra's son by Julius Caesar, Ptolemy XV Caesarion, was put to death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon in 49 BCE, his assassination in 44 BCE and the wars of the Second Triumvirate ended in Octavian's decisive victory over Mark Antony and <b>Cleopatra VII</b> at the Battle of Actium in 31 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Octavian defeated Antony and <b>Cleopatra VII</b> at Actium in 31 BCE and annexed Egypt in 30 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cleopatra VII</b> made her son Caesarion, by her account the son of Julius Caesar, co-ruler of Egypt as Ptolemy XV.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-2">2 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Bocchus II bequeathed the kingdom to Rome, Augustus installed Juba II in 25 BCE, and Juba married Cleopatra Selene, daughter of <b>Cleopatra VII</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-41" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cleopatra VII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-41">What was here in 41 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ptolemy-xii-auletes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ptolemy XII Auletes</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 117–51 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mark-antony.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mark Antony</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 83–30 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/caesarion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/caesarion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Caesarion</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 47–30 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cleopatra-selene-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Cleopatra Selene II</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 40 BCE – c. 5 BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nothing could part us whilst we lived, but death seems to threaten to divide us. You, a Roman born, have found a grave in Egypt; I, an Egyptian, am to seek that favor, and none but that, in your country.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Antony &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; at Antony's tomb, a captive of Octavian; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
